SFS) with a singularly perturbed differential system (SPDS) of the form:

  \begin{align}
  \varepsilon^2 x'' - \varepsilon y' + xy &= 0, \label{eq1}\\
  y' &= x^2 + 1. \label{eq2}
  \end{align}

  With $\varepsilon$ being a small positive number, this system models a nonlinear Euler-Bernoulli beam with a small time constant in the Kirchhoff plate equation. Our main objective is to establish the existence of a quasi-periodic solution with a specified frequency for the given SPDS when $\varepsilon$ is sufficiently small. To this end, we first transform the system into a first-order system and then apply the Poincar\'e map to a surface defined by the solution of the system at a specific time. Next, we determine the location of the image point under this map for a specific initial condition. Subsequently, we establish the conditions under which the Poincar\'e map has a fixed point within a specified region. Finally, we demonstrate that this fixed point corresponds to a quasi-periodic solution of the given SPDS.
